🔹 What is JIRA?
JIRA is a project management tool used for tracking tasks, issues, and Agile development. It helps teams collaborate, plan, and execute projects efficiently.

🔹 Key Concepts to Learn
1️⃣ Basics of JIRA
Understanding JIRA Software vs. JIRA Service Management vs. JIRA Work Management
JIRA Dashboard – Homepage where you see your tasks, projects, and updates.
JIRA Issues – Everything in JIRA (tasks, bugs, stories, etc.) is called an Issue.
2️⃣ JIRA Project Structure
Projects – A container for your tasks, bugs, or epics.
Issues – Each task, bug, or story is an Issue.
Issue Types
Story – A feature or requirement.
Task – A work item to complete.
Bug – A defect or issue that needs fixing.
Epic – A large work item that includes multiple stories/tasks.
3️⃣ Working with Issues
Creating Issues – How to create tasks, bugs, or stories.
Assigning Issues – Assign issues to team members.
Adding Comments & Attachments – Collaborate on issues.
Tracking Issue Status – Open, In Progress, Done, etc.
4️⃣ JIRA Agile Boards (Scrum & Kanban)
Scrum Board – Used for Sprint-based projects.
Kanban Board – Used for continuous work management.
Sprints & Backlog
Adding tasks to a sprint.
Moving tasks across To Do → In Progress → Done.
5️⃣ JIRA Workflows
Statuses – Track progress (To Do, In Progress, Done).
Transitions – Movement of issues between statuses.
6️⃣ JIRA Reports & Dashboards
Burndown Chart – Shows progress in a Sprint.
Velocity Chart – Measures completed work.
Sprint Reports – Helps track team performance.
7️⃣ JIRA Permissions & Roles
Admins – Manage users and settings.
Developers – Work on assigned tasks.
Testers – Verify and report issues.
8️⃣ Integrations & Automation
JIRA + GitLab, GitHub, Bitbucket – Link commits and branches.
Automation Rules – Auto-assign issues, send notifications, etc.
